<h2>Inspections for Home Buyers and Sellers</h2>
<p>Real estate transactions are a common reason for a roof inspection in Temple. A general home inspector covers the whole house, and a roofing contractor's inspection can add a closer look at the roof specifically.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Buyers</strong> benefit from knowing the roof's approximate condition and any needed repairs before closing, which can inform negotiations and future budgeting.</li>
<li><strong>Sellers</strong> can address issues ahead of listing, so a surprise does not show up in the buyer's inspection report and derail the timeline.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep in mind that we are a roofing contractor, so our inspection focuses on the roofing system and related components. It does not replace a full home inspection.</p>

<h2>Post-Storm Inspections</h2>
<p>Hail damage is often invisible from the ground, and wind damage can be as subtle as shingles that lifted and resealed with broken adhesive bonds. After a storm, an inspection with photos gives you a clear record of conditions. If you are considering an insurance claim, documentation helps you have an informed conversation with your insurer; our <a href="/services/storm-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">storm damage roof repair</a> and <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claim-assistance-temple-tx/">insurance claim assistance</a> pages explain what we can and cannot do in that process. Before we arrive, our <a href="/tools/storm-damage-checklist/">storm damage checklist</a> walks you through what to note from the ground.</p>

<div class="callout callout--warn"><strong>Stay off the roof.</strong> Shingles can be slick, damaged areas may not support weight, and falls from roofs cause serious injuries. Look from the ground, use binoculars, or take photos from a window. Leave the up-close work to us.</div>

<h2>Annual Inspections and Local Weather</h2>
<p>Temple and the rest of Bell County see a busy spring storm season from roughly March through June, a secondary round of storms in the fall, long summer stretches near or above 100 °F, and the occasional hard freeze. Each brings different wear. A yearly inspection, often scheduled in late winter or early spring before storm season, gives you a baseline. If a storm hits later, you have "before" photos to compare against. Our article on <a href="/blog/how-often-roof-inspection-temple-tx/">how often you should have your roof inspected</a> goes deeper, and the <a href="/blog/roof-maintenance-checklist-temple-homeowners/">roof maintenance checklist</a> covers what you can safely do between visits.</p>
